HACKING.adoc-18-Usually, this branch wants to be based on `trunk`. If your changes must be on a
HACKING.adoc-19-specific release, use its release branch (*not* the release tag) instead. For
HACKING.adoc-20-example, to make a fix for 4.11.1, base your branch on *4.11* (not on *4.11.1*).
HACKING.adoc:21:The `configure` step for the compiler recognises a development build from the
HACKING.adoc-22-`+dev` in the version number (see file `VERSION`), and release tarballs and the tagged Git commits do
HACKING.adoc-23-not have this which causes some important development things to be disabled
HACKING.adoc-24-(ocamltest and converting C compiler warnings to errors).
HACKING.adoc-25-
HACKING.adoc-26-2. Consult link:INSTALL.adoc[] for build instructions. Here is the gist of it:
HACKING.adoc-27-+
HACKING.adoc-28-----
HACKING.adoc:29:./configure
HACKING.adoc-30-make -j 4
HACKING.adoc-31-----
HACKING.adoc-32-If you are on a release build and need development options, you can add
HACKING.adoc:33:`--enable-ocamltest` (to allow running the testsuite) and `--enable-warn-error`
HACKING.adoc-34-(so you don't get caught by CI later!).
HACKING.adoc-35-
HACKING.adoc-36-3. Try the newly built compiler binaries `ocamlc`, `ocamlopt` or their
HACKING.adoc-37-`.opt` version. To try the toplevel, use:
HACKING.adoc-38-+
HACKING.adoc-39-----
HACKING.adoc-40-make runtop
HACKING.adoc-41-----
HACKING.adoc-42-
HACKING.adoc-43-4. Hack frenetically and keep rebuilding.
HACKING.adoc-44-
HACKING.adoc:45:5. Run the testsuite from time to time.
HACKING.adoc-46-+
HACKING.adoc-47-----
HACKING.adoc-48-make tests
HACKING.adoc-49-----
HACKING.adoc-50-
HACKING.adoc-51-6. You did it, Well done! Consult link:CONTRIBUTING.md[] to send your contribution upstream.
HACKING.adoc-52-
HACKING.adoc-53-See also our <<tips,development tips and tricks>>, for example on how to
HACKING.adoc-54-<<opam-switch,create an opam switch>> to test your modified compiler.
HACKING.adoc-55-
HACKING.adoc-56-=== What to do
HACKING.adoc-57-
HACKING.adoc-58-There is always a lot of potential tasks, both for old and
HACKING.adoc-59-newcomers. Here are various potential projects:
HACKING.adoc-60-
HACKING.adoc-61-* https://github.com/ocaml/ocaml/issues[The OCaml
HACKING.adoc-62-  bugtracker] contains reported bugs and feature requests. Some
HACKING.adoc-63-  changes that should be accessible to newcomers are marked with the
--
HACKING.adoc-162-
HACKING.adoc-163-and then opening link:./api_docgen/ocamldoc/build/html/libref/index.html[] in a web browser.
HACKING.adoc-164-The documentation is located in
HACKING.adoc-165-link:./api_docgen/odoc/build/html/libref/index.html[] when `--with-odoc` is
HACKING.adoc:166:passed to the configure script.
HACKING.adoc-167-
HACKING.adoc-168-=== Tools
HACKING.adoc-169-
HACKING.adoc-170-link:lex/[]:: The `ocamllex` lexer generator.
HACKING.adoc-171-
HACKING.adoc-172-link:yacc/[]:: The `ocamlyacc` parser generator. We do not recommend
HACKING.adoc-173-using it for user projects in need of a parser generator. Please
HACKING.adoc-174-consider using and contributing to
HACKING.adoc-175-link:http://gallium.inria.fr/~fpottier/menhir/[menhir] instead, which
HACKING.adoc-176-has tons of extra features, lets you write more readable grammars, and
HACKING.adoc-177-has excellent documentation.
