HACKING.adoc-221-[#tips]
HACKING.adoc-222-== Development tips and tricks
HACKING.adoc-223-
HACKING.adoc-224-=== Keep merge commits when merging and cherry-picking Github PRs
HACKING.adoc-225-
HACKING.adoc-226-Having the Github PR number show up in the git log is very useful for
HACKING.adoc-227-later triaging. We recently disabled the "Rebase and merge" button,
HACKING.adoc-228-precisely because it does not produce a merge commit.
HACKING.adoc-229-
HACKING.adoc-230-When you cherry-pick a PR in another branch, please cherry-pick this
HACKING.adoc-231-merge-style commit rather than individual commits, whenever
--
HACKING.adoc-275-
HACKING.adoc-276-===== Initial setup
HACKING.adoc-277-
HACKING.adoc-278-For the rest of the section to work, you'll need your compiler to be
HACKING.adoc:279:configured in the same way as `opam` would have configured it. The simplest
HACKING.adoc-280-way is to run the normal commands for the switch initialization, with the extra
HACKING.adoc-281-`--inplace-build` flag:
HACKING.adoc-282-
HACKING.adoc-283------
HACKING.adoc-284-opam switch create . --empty
HACKING.adoc-285-opam install . --inplace-build
HACKING.adoc-286------
HACKING.adoc-287-
HACKING.adoc:288:However, if you need specific configuration options, you can also configure it
HACKING.adoc:289:manually, as long as you make sure that the configuration prefix is the one
HACKING.adoc-290-where `opam` would install the compiler.
HACKING.adoc-291-You will then need to install the compiler, either from the working directory
HACKING.adoc-292-(that you must build yourself) or using the regular sandboxed builds.
HACKING.adoc-293-
HACKING.adoc-294------
HACKING.adoc-295-# Example with regular opam build
HACKING.adoc-296-opam switch create . --empty
HACKING.adoc-297-opam install .
HACKING.adoc:298:./configure --prefix=$(opam var prefix) # put extra configuration args here
HACKING.adoc-299------
HACKING.adoc-300-
HACKING.adoc-301------
HACKING.adoc-302-# Example with installation from the current directory
HACKING.adoc-303-opam switch create . --empty
HACKING.adoc:304:./configure --prefix=$(opam var prefix) # put extra configuration args here
HACKING.adoc-305-make -j
HACKING.adoc-306-opam install . --assume-built
HACKING.adoc-307------
HACKING.adoc-308-
HACKING.adoc-309-===== Basic workflow
HACKING.adoc-310-
HACKING.adoc-311-We will assume that the workflow alternates between work on the compiler and
HACKING.adoc-312-external (`opam`-related) commands.
HACKING.adoc-313-As an example, debugging an issue in the compiler can be done by a first step
HACKING.adoc-314-that triggers the issue (by installing a given `opam` package), then adding
HACKING.adoc-315-some logging to the compiler, re-trigger the issue, and based on the logs either
HACKING.adoc-316-add more logging, or try a patch, and so on.
HACKING.adoc-317-
HACKING.adoc-318-The part of this workflow that we're going to optimize is when we switch from
HACKING.adoc-319-working on the compiler to using the compiler. The basic way to do this is to
HACKING.adoc-320-run `opam install .` again, but this will recompile the compiler from scratch
HACKING.adoc-321-and also trigger a recompilation of all the packages in the switch.
HACKING.adoc-322-
--
HACKING.adoc-365-Full example:
HACKING.adoc-366-
HACKING.adoc-367------
HACKING.adoc-368-opam switch create . --empty
HACKING.adoc:369:./configure --prefix=$(opam var prefix) --disable-ocamldoc --disable-ocamltest
HACKING.adoc:370:make world && make opt
HACKING.adoc-371-opam custom-install ocaml-variants -- make install
HACKING.adoc-372-opam reinstall --forget-pending --yes
HACKING.adoc-373-opam install ocaml
HACKING.adoc-374-# You now have a working switch, in which you can start installing packages
HACKING.adoc-375------
HACKING.adoc-376-
HACKING.adoc-377-One advantage of this plugin over a plain `make install` is that it
HACKING.adoc-378-correctly tracks the files associated with the compiler, so if your
HACKING.adoc-379-`make install` command only installs the bytecode versions of the tools,
HACKING.adoc-380-then with `opam-custom-install` you will end up in a state where only the
HACKING.adoc-381-bytecode tools are installed, whereas with a raw `make install` you will have
HACKING.adoc-382-stale native binaries remaining in your switch.
HACKING.adoc-383-Since it's significantly faster to build the bytecode version of the tools,
HACKING.adoc-384-and many `opam` packages will pick the native version of the compilers if
HACKING.adoc-385-present and the bytecode version otherwise, you can build your initial switch
HACKING.adoc-386-with the native versions (to get quickly to a state where a bug appears),
HACKING.adoc-387-then clean your working directory and start building bytecode tools only
HACKING.adoc-388-for the actual debugging phase.
--
HACKING.adoc-416-`make partialclean`:: Clean the OCaml files but keep the compiled C files.
HACKING.adoc-417-
HACKING.adoc-418-`make depend`:: Regenerate the `.depend` file. Should be used each time new dependencies are added between files.
HACKING.adoc-419-
HACKING.adoc:420:`make -C testsuite parallel`:: see link:testsuite/HACKING.adoc[]
HACKING.adoc-421-
HACKING.adoc-422-You can use `make foo V=1` to build the target foo and show full
HACKING.adoc-423-commands instead of abbreviated names like OCAMLC, etc. This can be
HACKING.adoc-424-useful to know the flags to use to manually rebuild a file.
HACKING.adoc-425-
HACKING.adoc-426-Additionally, there are some developer specific targets in link:Makefile.dev[].
HACKING.adoc-427-These targets are automatically available when working in a Git clone of the
HACKING.adoc-428-repository, but are not available from a tarball.
HACKING.adoc-429-
HACKING.adoc:430:=== Automatic configure options
HACKING.adoc-431-
HACKING.adoc:432:If you have options to `configure` which you always (or at least frequently)
HACKING.adoc:433:use, it's possible to store them in Git, and `configure` will automatically add
HACKING.adoc-434-them. For example, you may wish to avoid building the debug runtime by default
HACKING.adoc-435-while developing, in which case you can issue
HACKING.adoc:436:`git config --global ocaml.configure '--disable-debug-runtime'`. The `configure`
HACKING.adoc-437-script will alert you that it has picked up this option and added it _before_
HACKING.adoc:438:any options you specified for `configure`.
HACKING.adoc-439-
HACKING.adoc-440-Options are added before those passed on the command line, so it's possible to
HACKING.adoc:441:override them, for example `./configure --enable-debug-runtime` will build the
HACKING.adoc-442-debug runtime, since the enable flag appears after the disable flag. You can
HACKING.adoc-443-also use the full power of Git's `config` command and have options specific to
HACKING.adoc-444-particular clone or worktree.
HACKING.adoc-445-
HACKING.adoc:446:=== Speeding up configure
HACKING.adoc-447-
HACKING.adoc:448:`configure` includes the standard `-C` option which caches various test results
HACKING.adoc-449-in the file `config.cache` and can use those results to avoid running tests in
HACKING.adoc-450-subsequent invocations. This mechanism works fine, except that it is easy to
HACKING.adoc-451-clean the cache by mistake (e.g. with `git clean -dfX`). The cache is also
HACKING.adoc:452:host-specific which means the file has to be deleted if you run `configure` with
HACKING.adoc:453:a new `--host` value (this is quite common on Windows, where `configure` is
HACKING.adoc-454-also quite slow to run).
HACKING.adoc-455-
HACKING.adoc-456-You can elect to have host-specific cache files by issuing
HACKING.adoc:457:`git config --global ocaml.configure-cache .`. The `configure` script will now
HACKING.adoc-458-automatically create `ocaml-host.cache` (e.g. `ocaml-x86_64-pc-windows.cache`,
HACKING.adoc-459-or `ocaml-default.cache`). If you work with multiple worktrees, you can share
HACKING.adoc:460:these cache files by issuing `git config --global ocaml.configure-cache ..`. The
HACKING.adoc:461:directory is interpreted _relative_ to the `configure` script.
HACKING.adoc-462-
